==Distribution==
There are about 70 species native to southern and eastern Africa and the Arabian peninsula.<ref name="POWO_10323-1">{{cite web |title=''Osteospermum'' L. |work=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:10323-1 |access-date=2020-07-19 |archive-date=2020-08-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200815174157/http://www.plantsoftheworldonline.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:10323-1 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Cultivation==
''Osteospermum'' are popular in cultivation, where they are frequently used in [[Bedding (horticulture)|summer bedding]] schemes in parks and gardens. Numerous [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrids]] and cultivars have been grown with a wide range of tropical colors. Yellow cultivars tend to have a yellow center (sometimes off-white).

Plants prefer a warm and sunny position and rich soil, although they tolerate poor soil, salt or drought well. Modern cultivars flower continuously when watered and fertilised well, and dead-heading is not necessary, because they do not set seed easily. 
If planted in a container, soil should be prevented from drying out completely. If they do, the plants will go into "sleep mode" and survive the period of drought, but they will abort their flower buds and not easily come back into flower. Moreover, roots are relatively susceptible to rotting if watered too profusely after the dry period.

===Cultivars===
Most widely sold cultivars are grown as annuals, are mainly hybrids of ''O. jucundum'', ''O. ecklonis'' and ''O. grandiflorum'' and can be hardy to -2&nbsp;°C (30&nbsp;°F). If hardy, they can be grown as perennials or as shrubs.

